 Gradient Scaling is a technique used in differentiable volume rendering to mitigate near-camera overfitting and the formation of "floaters" during radiance field optimization. It addresses the problem of gradient imbalance along rays, where samples far from the camera receive significantly smaller gradients than those near the camera due to the exponential decay of transmittance. Unlike Distortion Loss with Power Transformation, which modifies the loss function to penalize metric distance, Gradient Scaling modifies the optimization process itself by rescaling gradients during backpropagation to ensure uniform gradient magnitude across all samples on a ray.
 
+### Weight Regularization
+Weight Regularization is a loss term applied to NeRF weights to suppress geometric noise and remove cloudy artifacts near corners or geometric boundaries by encouraging a more concentrated density distribution [S2]. Without this regularization, results exhibit severe geometry noise and cloudy artifacts [S2]. Unlike mip-NeRF 360 which optimizes distances, this method penalizes pairs with scattered large weights to suppress noise peaks and aggregate weights to the correct object surface [S2]. The loss is defined as $\mathcal{L}_{reg} = \sum_{\mathbf{I}_{tgt}} \sum_{\mathbf{r}} \sum_{(i,j) \in K} w_i w_j |d_i - d_j|$, where $w_k$ is the contribution to the final ray color and $(i,j)$ are pairs of samples with distances $\|d_i - d_j\|$ [S2].
